<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maritsa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Evros, Hebrus, Meriç</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maritsa, called Evros in Greek, Meriç in Turkish and Hebrus in antiquity, is the longest river lying wholly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, about 480 kilometres long. It rises in the Rila mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, flows through the Thracian plain past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, and forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> before entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> near the ancient city of Aenus, modern Enez.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maritsa.jpg" alt="Maritsa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Preslav~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maritsaorigin2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
